QB.add(35,[{"n":1,"q":"A 38-year-old male, a known alcoholic, presents with intermittent epigastric pain for three months, described as a burning sensation occurring mainly after meals. He reports bloating, occasional nausea, and temporary relief with antacids. Upper GI endoscopy reveals peptic ulcers in the gastric antrum. What is the most likely cause of this patient’s gastritis?","o":[{"t":"Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells"},{"t":"Helicobacter pylori infection"},{"t":"Chronic alcohol consumption alone"},{"t":"Stress-related mucosal injury"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient’s symptoms, including epigastric pain, bloating, and nausea, combined with the presence of peptic ulcers at the antrum on endoscopy, are most consistent with Type B gastritis . In this case, the underlying cause of the peptic ulcers is an infection with Helicobacter pylori."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Type B Gastritis vs. Type A Gastritis:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Feature Type B Gastritis Type A Gastritis"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Cause Primarily caused by H. pylori Autoimmune condition, often"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"infection and long-term NSAID use associated with autoantibodies"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"against parietal cells and intrinsic factor"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Location of Antrum (lower part) of the stomach, Fundus (upper part) of the stomach, Inflammatio near the pylorus where parietal cells are found n"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Symptoms Epigastric pain, bloating, nausea, Often asymptomatic, but may cause"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"vomiting, and ulceration in some symptoms like nausea, vomiting, cases. and abdominal discomfort"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Associated Peptic ulcer disease (PUD), gastric Pernicious anemia (due to vitamin Conditions cancer risk B12 deficiency)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Histological Chronic inflammation, increased Chronic inflammation, loss of Findings lymphocytes and neutrophils, often parietal cells, and often atrophy of"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"with atrophic changes the gastric mucosa"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Risk Factor H. pylori infection, NSAIDs, alcohol, Autoimmunity, genetic s smoking predisposition, vitamin B12"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"deficiency"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Treatment Eradication of H. pylori with Vitamin B12 supplementation and"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"antibiotics and proton pump immune modulation; PPIs may also inhibitors (PPIs) be used"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Prognosis If untreated, it can lead to peptic If untreated, it can result in"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"ulcers and an increased risk of pernicious anemia and an increased gastric cancer. risk of gastric carcinoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Autoimmune destruction of parietal cells (Option A): This usually causes Type A gastritis, which is caused by an autoimmune process affecting the stomach’s parietal cells, often resulting in vitamin B12 deficiency."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Chronic alcohol consumption alone (Option C): While alcohol can irritate the stomach lining and contribute to gastritis, it is less likely to be the sole cause of peptic ulcers in this patient."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Stress-related mucosal injury (Option D): This type of gastritis is typically associated with severe physical stress, such as trauma, surgery, or burns. It does not align with this patient's chronicity or risk factors."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 45-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of epigastric discomfort, bloating, and occasional nausea. An endoscopic gastric biopsy specimen reveals the following characteristic organism, as shown below. The patient is diagnosed with chronic gastritis. Which type of gastritis is least likely to be associated with this infection?","o":[{"t":"Antral predominant gastritis"},{"t":"Non-atrophic pangastritis"},{"t":"Corpus predominant gastritis"},{"t":"Granulomatous gastritis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Helicobacter pylori is a common cause of chronic gastritis and is typically associated with the following types:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Antral predominant gastritis: Most common in early H. pylori infection, affecting the antrum of the stomach."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-atrophic pangastritis: H. pylori can affect the entire stomach, but in the early stages, it usually does not cause atrophy."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Corpus-predominant gastritis: It may develop as the infection progresses, often leading to atrophic changes in the stomach's corpus (body)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"However, granulomatous gastritis is not associated with Helicobacter pylori infection. It is more commonly seen in conditions like Crohn’s disease, sarcoidosis, or infections like tuberculosis. It is characterized by granuloma formation rather than the chronic inflammation typical of H. pylori-associated gastritis ."}],"qi":[6441]},{"n":3,"q":"A 55-year-old male presents with epigastric pain that worsens with meals and improves with antacids. Urea breath test is positive. Which of the following conditions is the patient least likely to be at increased risk for?","o":[{"t":"Gastric adenocarcinoma"},{"t":"MALT lymphoma"},{"t":"Esophageal squamous cell carcinoma"},{"t":"Duodenal ulcers"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"A positive urea breath test indicates H. pylori infection, which is associated with gastric adenocarcinoma, MALT lymphoma, and duodenal ulcers; while it plays a protective role in esophageal adenocarcinoma."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Complications associated with Peptic Ulcer Disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bleeding"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Perforation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Duodenal and gastric ulcers"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Malignancies: Gastric adenocarcinoma: Gastric ulcer >3 cm is a premalignant condition that can progress to adenocarcinoma. MALT lymphoma: 60-90% of patients - Complete remission on eradication of H. pylori infection."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gastric adenocarcinoma: Gastric ulcer >3 cm is a premalignant condition that can progress to adenocarcinoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MALT lymphoma: 60-90% of patients - Complete remission on eradication of H. pylori infection."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gastric adenocarcinoma: Gastric ulcer >3 cm is a premalignant condition that can progress to adenocarcinoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MALT lymphoma: 60-90% of patients - Complete remission on eradication of H. pylori infection."},{"k":"img","v":6464},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Conditions where H. pylori is protective: The mechanism underlying this protective effect is likely H. pylori–induced hypochlorhydria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"GERD"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Barrett’s esophagus"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Esophageal adenocarcinoma (Option C)"}]},{"n":4,"q":"A 42-year-old man presents with a 4-week history of burning epigastric pain that occurs 1-2 hours after meals and is temporarily relieved by food. He also complains of vomiting. On examination, his heart rate is 115 beats per minute, and his temperature is 98.6°F. Mild epigastric tenderness is noted, and laboratory tests show mild anemia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Duodenal ulcer"},{"t":"Acute pancreatitis"},{"t":"Gastric ulcer"},{"t":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The patient's characteristic epigastric pain relieved by food, vomiting, tachycardia and mild anemia suggests duodenal ulcer ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastric Ulcer (GU) Duodenal Ulcer (DU)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common risk factors for both GU and DU: H. pylori, indiscriminate use of NSAIDS due to comorbidities in the patients like osteoarthritis or requiring total knee replacement, etc."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"> 6th-decade Basal/Nocturnal acid production: › Premalignant condition. MC site: First part of duodenum or duodenal MC site: Lesser curvature of stomach. cap, usually within 3 cm of the pylorus."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"Size of Ulcer: <1 cm."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Pain Pattern: Pain is precipitated by food. Pain Pattern: Pain occurs 90 min to 3 h after a"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"meal and is relieved by food or antacids."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common Features: Epigastric pain (gnawing or burning sensation) Epigastric tenderness Tachycardia and Orthostasis caused by: Vomiting, may be due to pylorospasm or local edema GI bleeding Rigidity in the anterior abdominal wall is suggestive of perforation Succussion splash: Gastric outlet obstruction (due to pyloric stenosis, pylorospasm, or local edema due to ulcer in the peripyloric area or fibrosis extending to the duodenum)."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Acute pancreatitis (Option B): In pancreatitis, eating fatty food typically exacerbates the pain, causing a sudden onset of severe, sharp, and persistent epigastric discomfort that may radiate to the back. It is accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and elevated pancreatic enzymes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastric ulcer (Option C): Although gastric ulcers can cause epigastric pain, the patient's pain pattern ( occurring after meals and relieved by food ) is more characteristic of a duodenal ulcer."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) (Option D): GERD primarily causes heartburn and regurgitation, with no significant vomiting or the pain relief pattern seen in this patient"}]},{"n":5,"q":"A patient is suspected to have peptic ulcer disease and endoscopy is performed to confirm the diagnosis. Which of the following endoscopic findings is most consistent with peptic ulcer disease?","o":[{"t":""},{"t":""},{"t":""},{"t":""}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Endoscopy findings are suggestive of duodenal ulcers. Duodenal ulcers have a clean base with no recent hemorrhages."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Workup for H.pylori:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Noninvasive Test: For initial diagnosis and eradication"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Do a test after 4 weeks of stopping antibiotics."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Before the test, stop antisecretory agents 7 days prior."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The first test offered."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Risk of false negatives in people using antibiotics in recent times."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Invasive Test: Upper GI Endoscopy is performed to obtain a biopsy"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Endoscopy findings may reveal ulcers with a clean base with no recent hemorrhages."},{"k":"img","v":6471},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Note:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"If the patient is not willing for an endoscopy, the non-invasive approach is the best. Both UBT and UREASE can be false negatives due to recent use of:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Surface protective agent"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Reduces the acid mucosa contact time"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Helps in the healing of ulcers. IOC to document PUD - UGIE >> Barium studies of proximal gut."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IOC to document PUD - UGIE >> Barium studies of proximal gut."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"IOC to document PUD - UGIE >> Barium studies of proximal gut."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastric antral vascular ectasia (GAVE) (Option A): Endoscopic findings of GAVE include characteristic longitudinal red streaks in the antrum resembling “watermelon stripes”."},{"k":"img","v":6477},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Dieulafoy's lesion (Option B): Endoscopic findings of Dieulafoy's lesion include a pinpoint, bleeding arterial vessel within normal-appearing mucosa."},{"k":"img","v":6482},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastric Polyps (Option D): Gastric polyps on endoscopy typically appear as raised, well-defined lesions, which can vary in size and shape, and may be sessile or pedunculated."},{"k":"img","v":6488}],"m":1},{"n":6,"q":"A 45-year-old male presents to the emergency department with hematemesis and melena for the past 2 days. He reports long-term use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) for chronic back pain. On examination, he is found to be pale and hypotensive. Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy reveals a bleeding duodenal ulcer, as shown below. Which artery is most likely involved in the bleeding?","o":[{"t":"Left gastric artery"},{"t":"Gastroduodenal artery"},{"t":"Splenic artery"},{"t":"Common hepatic artery"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This patient presents with features of a bleeding duodenal ulcer as shown in the endoscopy, likely complicated by his long-term NSAID use."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastroduodenal artery is the most common artery involved in bleeding from duodenal ulcers."},{"k":"img","v":6490},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Endoscopic image of a bleeding duodenal ulcer, indicated by the black arrow."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Gastric Ulcer Duodenal Ulcer"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Common risk factors for both GU and DU: H.pylori and indiscriminate use of NSAIDs. Epigastric pain, described as a burning or gnawing discomfort, can be present in both DU and GU Epigastric tenderness Tachycardia or Orthostasis: Vomiting may be due to pylorospasm or local edema."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The pain pattern in GU patients: Discomfort may be The typical pain pattern in DU occurs 90 min to 3 hours precipitated by food. after a meal and is frequently relieved by antacids or"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"food."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"M/C site: Lesser curvature M/C site: The first part of the duodenum or duodenal"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"cap is usually within 3 cm of the pylorus."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Artery involved: Left gastric artery (Option A) Artery involved: Gastroduodenal artery (Option B)"},{"k":"img","v":6496},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"gastric ulcer\" data-author=\"Ed Uthman, MD\" data-hash=\"9387\" data-license=\"Open Access\" data-source=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Be nign_gastric_ulcer_1.jpg\" data-tags=\"April2025\""},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"P3DPHEa41744372907.png\" />"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"PUD Related Complications:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Gastrointestinal bleeding: (most common)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"GU: Left gastric artery"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DU: Gastroduodenal artery: Torrential hemorrhage contributes to potential catastrophic or hemodynamic instability."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"› In elderly patients, due to concomitant NSAIDS usage (COX-1 inhibitor fi fl Protective PG production fi fl Stomach defence mechanism)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"30-day mortality: 2.5 - 10 %"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leading cause of death - Nonbleeding causes (MODS, pulmonary complications due to long-term rest, DVT, PE, and may develop RDS, metastases to liver, and other distant organs)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Perforation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"30-day mortality: >20% ·"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Clinical manifestations: Acute abdominal pain + tachycardia + Guarding and Rigidity of anterior abdominal wall."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"X-ray abdomen or USG shows free fluid in the abdomen."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"NOTE:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anterior DU has a higher chance of perforation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Posterior DU has a higher chance of bleeding."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Gastric Outlet Obstruction (Least common)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ulcer-related local inflammation and local edema triggers pylorospasm."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Location: Peripyloric area, duodenal ulcers."}],"qi":[6448]},{"n":7,"q":"All of the following regimens can be used in managing H. pylori eradication except?","o":[{"t":"Triple therapy: Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) + Amoxicillin + Clarithromycin"},{"t":"Quadruple therapy: PPI + Bismuth subsalicylate + Metronidazole + Tetracycline"},{"t":"Dual therapy: Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) + Erythromycin"},{"t":"Hybrid therapy: PPI + Amoxicillin + Clarithromycin + Nitroimidazole"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"H. pylori eradication is essential in the management of peptic ulcer disease (PUD) The most common treatment regimens include combinations of a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) with antibiotics."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"1. Triple therapy: (Option A)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PPI for 4-6 weeks, Clarithromycin + Amoxicillin for 2 weeks."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"2. Quadruple therapy: (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PPI"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bismuth (Disadvantage: Blackish discoloration of the tongue)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Tetracyclines"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metronidazole"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"3. Hybrid therapy (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For the patients who cannot tolerate two antibiotics at a time."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PPI + Amoxicillin for 7 days o PPI + Amoxicillin + Clarithromycin + Nitroimidazole × 7 days."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"4. LOAD"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In patients with macrolides-based resistance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"L evofloxacin + O meprazole + A linia (Nitazoxanide) + D oxycycline."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Dual therapy: Proton pump inhibitor (PPI) + Erythromycin : Erythromycin is not effective against H.pylori."}]},{"n":8,"q":"A 50-year-old male presents with a 3-month history of intermittent epigastric pain, nausea, and bloating. He has a history of duodenal ulcers diagnosed 5 years ago, which were treated with antacids. He reports occasional indigestion. Upper gastrointestinal endoscopy reveals a small duodenal ulcer. A biopsy confirms the presence of H. pylori infection. Which of the following conditions in this patient would warrant H. pylori eradication therapy? 1) Peptic ulcer disease (PUD) 2) Gastric lymphoma (MALT lymphoma) 3) Non-ulcer dyspepsia 4) Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)","o":[{"t":"1, 2, 3, 4"},{"t":"2, 3, 4"},{"t":"1 and 2"},{"t":"Only 1"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"H. pylori eradication is primarily indicated in peptic ulcer disease and gastric MALT lymphoma cases ."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Indications of eradication of H.pylori:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Most important indications:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Peptic ulcer disease"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gastric MALT lymphoma"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Other indications:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"patients with atrophic gastritis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"first degree relatives of patients with gastric cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"patients with unexplained iron deficiency anaemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"patients with chronic idiopathic thrombocytopenic purpura"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"H.pylori eradication is not indicated in IBS and Non-ulcer dyspepsia."}]},{"n":9,"q":"A 45-year-old male with a history of peptic ulcer disease was treated with triple therapy (omeprazole, clarithromycin, and amoxicillin) for 14 days. Six weeks later, he presents with persistent epigastric discomfort. Which test is most appropriate to confirm the eradication of the causative organism","o":[{"t":"Urea breath test"},{"t":"H. pylori serology"},{"t":"Upper GI endoscopy"},{"t":"Rapid urease test"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The above-described case scenario is a condition caused by H. pylori, and the urea breath test (UBT) is the most appropriate non-invasive test to confirm the eradication of H. pylori after treatment."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Tests used to evaluate H.Pylori eradication"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Urea breath test It is a simple, non-invasive, highly sensitive and specific (Option A) test."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It is ideally performed at least 4 weeks after the completion of therapy to avoid any false negatives due to the residual effects of antibiotics or PPIs."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Stool antigen test It is also a noninvasive test, and to avoid false"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"negatives, it needs to be performed after 4 weeks of treatment ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Biopsy-based histology tests It can be done to evaluate the success of the treatment."},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"It can only be performed after 2 weeks of stopping PPIs and 6 weeks of stopping antibiotics. It is not commonly preferred."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"H. pylori serology (Option B) It is not a preferred test because this test detects antibodies against H. pylori and after treatment, it may remain positive even if the infection has been eradicated, making it unsuitable for confirming eradication."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Upper GI endoscopy (Option C) is not routinely required to confirm H. pylori eradication as this procedure is invasive and is not indicated in this case unless there are other red flags or complications."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Rapid urease test (Option D), is an invasive test which is useful for diagnosing active infection during an endoscopy and generally not needed for routine post-treatment confirmation of eradication."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A 48-year-old female with a history of rheumatoid arthritis has been taking naproxen for the past 6 months. She presents with epigastric pain, nausea, and dark stools. Upper GI endoscopy reveals a gastric ulcer. What is the drug of choice for managing this condition?","o":[{"t":"Misoprostol"},{"t":"Sucralfate"},{"t":"Proton pump inhibitors"},{"t":"H2-receptor antagonists"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The best option for treating NSAID-induced gastric ulcers is Proton Pump Inhibitors (PPIs) , such as omeprazole or pantoprazole, as it helps to heal the ulcer, relieve pain, and promote faster mucosal recovery."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"They reduce gastric acid production by inhibiting the H+/K+ ATPase (proton pump) in the parietal cells."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of NSAID induced gastric/ duodenal ulcer:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Firstly, NSAID should be discontinued and they should be treated with PPIs or H2 receptor antagonists."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In case of conditions where NSAID cannot be stopped, PPI is the drug of choice."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"For prophylactic management (in case of risk factors) Misoprostol Selective COX-2 inhibitor PPI is used"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Misoprostol"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Selective COX-2 inhibitor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PPI is used"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Misoprostol"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Selective COX-2 inhibitor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"PPI is used"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Risk factors for NSAID-induced gastric/ duodenal ulcer:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Advanced age"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"History of PUD"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Concomitant use of Glucocorticoids High dose NSAIDs Multiple NSAIDs SSRIs Anticoagulants or Clopidogrel"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucocorticoids"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High dose NSAIDs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple NSAIDs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SSRIs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticoagulants or Clopidogrel"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Concomitant Infection with H. pylori"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cigarette Smoking"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Alcohol usage"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glucocorticoids"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High dose NSAIDs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple NSAIDs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"SSRIs"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anticoagulants or Clopidogrel"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Misoprostol (Option A) is a prostaglandin analog that prevents NSAID-induced ulcers , hence used for prophylaxis. However, it is not the first-line treatment for active ulcers as it is less effective than PPIs in promoting ulcer healing."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sucralfate (Option B) is a cytoprotective agent that forms a protective barrier over the ulcer, but it is not as effective as PPIs in promoting ulcer healing."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"H2-receptor antagonists (Option D) reduce gastric acid secretion, and help in healing the gastric ulcer, however in the given condition as she is suffering from arthritis, she is likely to continue NSAIDs and H2-receptor antagonists is not preferred in this condition."}]},{"n":11,"q":"A 52-year-old male with a history of peptic ulcer disease (PUD) is prescribed omeprazole, for the management of his condition. Which of the following is not an anticipated adverse effect of long-term PPI use?","o":[{"t":"Stress fractures"},{"t":"Community acquired pneumonia"},{"t":"Chronic kidney disease"},{"t":"Hypermagnesemia"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypermagnesemia is not a known side effect of PPI use. Long-term PPI use is more commonly associated with hypomagnesemia ."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Adverse effects of long-term usage of PPI"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Acute interstitial nephritis Fever, eosinophilia, and decreased urine output leading (AIN) to Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Bone pain Long-term PPIs increase the risk of osteoporosis and (Option A ruled out) stress fractures."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Clostridium difficile infections Due to reduced gastric acidity , which normally acts as a"},{"k":"p","l":2,"v":"barrier to bacterial growth, there is increased risk of C. difficile infections"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Community-Acquired Increased incidence for CAP is seen due to impaired Pneumonia gastric defense , leading to increased bacterial (Option B ruled out) colonization in the upper GI tract."},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Chronic kidney disease Chronic or untreated AIN can progress to CKD over time. (Option C ruled out)"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Hypomagnesemia It can cause muscle cramps, tetany-like manifestations, (Option D) and arrhythmias."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A 28-year-old woman presents with the finding shown in the image and has a family history of similar lesions. Endoscopy reveals multiple hamartomatous polyps in the small intestine. Based on the clinical presentation, which of the following statements about the most probable condition is incorrect? a) Typically associated with a mutation in the STK11. b) Associated with bleeding and intussusception. c) Associated with alopecia, nail dystrophy, and malabsorption. d) Low or no risk of malignant transformation. e) Esophageal webs and glossitis are seen.","o":[{"t":"c, d, e"},{"t":"a, b"},{"t":"a, c, d"},{"t":"b, c, d, e"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The clinical features of multiple hamartomatous polyps in the small intestine, along with pigmentation around the mouth and a positive family history, point towards Peutz-Jeghers syndrome, which is associated with mutations in STK11, bleeding, and intussusception."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Peutz–Jeghers Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple hamartomatous polyps in the small intestine and colon."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Melanin pigmentation of the lips, mouth, and digits."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most cases are asymptomatic but may present with chronic bleeding, anemia, or intussusception. (Statement-b)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Significant risk of cancers: small bowel, colon, pancreas, lungs, testis, ovaries, breast, and endometrium. (Statement-d)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Autosomal dominant disorder, typically due to truncating mutations in the STK11 gene on chromosome 19p. (Statement-a)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diagnosis requires at least two of the following: Small bowel polyposis. Mucocutaneous pigmentation. Family history suggesting autosomal dominant inheritance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small bowel polyposis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mucocutaneous pigmentation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Family history suggesting autosomal dominant inheritance."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Small bowel polyposis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mucocutaneous pigmentation."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Family history suggesting autosomal dominant inheritance."},{"k":"img","v":6507},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cronkhite Canada Syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sporadic condition associated with hamartomatous polyps."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized by alopecia (hair loss), onychodystrophy (nail changes), and diarrhea (Statement-c)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It predominantly affects the Japanese population."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low or negligible risk of malignant transformation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glossitis & oesophagal webs (Statement-e) are more commonly associated with Plummer-Vinson syndrome."}],"qi":[6456]},{"n":13,"q":"A patient presents with features suggestive of Boerhaave syndrome. What is the characteristic triad seen in this condition?","o":[{"t":"Multiple episodes of vomiting + chest pain + subcutaneous emphysema (Mackler’s triad)"},{"t":"Retching + Acute epigastric pain + Inability to pass NG tube (Borchardt’s triad)"},{"t":"Aortic stenosis + Deficiency of vWF + Lower GI bleed (Heyde syndrome)"},{"t":"Jaundice + Right upper quadrant pain + UGI bleed (Quincke triad)"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Mackler’s triad (Vomiting, chest pain, and subcutaneous emphysema) is seen in Boerhaave syndrome."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Boerhaave’s Syndrome: It is a spontaneous esophageal rupture caused by a sudden increase in intraesophageal pressure, typically from vomiting against a closed glottis resulting in a left-sided esophageal perforation , which allows gastric contents to spill into the pleural cavity, leading to a left pleural effusion and subcutaneous emphysema ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Classic triad : Vomiting, chest pain, and subcutaneous emphysema. (Mackler’s triad)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms : May include fever, tachycardia, and tachypnea in the presence of sepsis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Physical exam : Hamman’s sign (crunching sound on auscultation due to surgical emphysema)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complications : Pneumothorax, mediastinitis, and sepsis."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Borchardt’s triad: Seen in Gastric volvulus (Option B)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Retching + Acute epigastric pain + Inability to pass NG tube."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Heyde syndrome (Option C) :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aortic stenosis + Deficiency of vWF + Lower GI bleed."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Quincke triad: Seen in patients with hemobilia secondary to a hepatic artery aneurysm (Option D)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Jaundice + Right upper quadrant pain + UGI bleed."}]},{"n":14,"q":"All of the following classification systems can be used in GI bleeding except?","o":[{"t":"Glasgow-Blatchford"},{"t":"Forrest"},{"t":"Sarin"},{"t":"Atlanta"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Atlanta classification is used for acute pancreatitis , not GI bleeding. It classifies the severity of pancreatitis, distinguishing between mild, moderate, and severe cases."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Glasgow-Blatchford score (Option A) is used to assess the severity of upper GI bleeding and predict the need for intervention or hospitalisation."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"It includes parameters like hemoglobin level, blood pressure, heart rate, and the presence of melena or syncope."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Forrest classification (Option B) is used to classify the appearance of ulcers during endoscopy and helps predict the risk of re-bleeding."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sarin classification (Option C) is used to assess the risk of bleeding from gastric varices , particularly in patients with portal hypertension . It helps in predicting the likelihood of variceal hemorrhage and the need for intervention."}]}]);
